</a></p><h2><a name=3D"ref60">8.6. Space</a></h2>
<p></p><p>
A space is a container for geometry objects.
It is similar to the rigid body "world", except for collision instead of
dynamics.</p><p>
The space does high level collision culling, which means that it can
identify which pairs of geometry objects are potentially touching.
You can safely call <a class=3D"func" href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/2=
0011224181047/http://www.q12.org/ode/ode-0.025-userguide.html#func_dCollide=
"><tt>dCollide()</tt></a> for only those pairs, instead of
having to call <a class=3D"func" href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/200112=
24181047/http://www.q12.org/ode/ode-0.025-userguide.html#func_dCollide"><tt=
>dCollide()</tt></a> for <i>every</i> object-object pair.
This can save a huge amount of time.
Various collision culling algorithms will be available, however only the
first is currently implemented:
</p><ul>
<li>	Simple <i>n</i><sup>2</sup> object intersections.
</li><li>	Oct tree.
</li><li>	Coordinate sorting.
</li><li>	Hash table?
</li></ul><p></p><p>
Here are the functions used for spaces:</p><p>
<a name=3D"func_dSpaceCreate"></a></p><pre><a name=3D"func_dSpaceCreate"><f=
ont color=3D"#0000ff">
dSpaceID dSpaceCreate();
</font></a></pre><blockquote><a name=3D"func_dSpaceCreate">
